      createBlock("1. The Origin: Rover the Dog in Microsoft Bob (1994)", "h3"),
      createBlock("To understand the tragedy of Comic Sans, we have to travel back to 1994. Microsoft typographer Vincent Connare was reviewing an early beta of a software suite called Microsoft Bob, which was designed explicitly for absolute novice computer users, mostly children. In the software, a cartoon dog named Rover guided users around. The problem? Rover spoke through speech bubbles set in formal, stern Times New Roman."),
      createBlock("Connare took one look and thought: 'Dogs don't talk in Times New Roman!' He was right. Inspired by the dynamic, hand-lettered text in legendary comic books like *Watchmen* and *The Dark Knight Returns*, he quickly sketched out Comic Sans as a playful, casual alternative specifically meant for cartoon speech balloons."),
      createBlock("2. The Sin of Misplaced Context", "h3"),
      createBlock("The disaster occurred shortly after. Microsoft made a fateful decision to include Comic Sans as a default system font in Windows 95. Suddenly, millions of untrained computer users worldwide gained access to this exceptionally friendly-looking font. Without typographic training, they began using it for absolutely everything."),
      createBlock("And I mean everything. People printed out angry eviction notices in Comic Sans. Hospitals used it for defibrillator instructions and, appallingly, cancer diagnoses. Academic research papers, corporate terminations, and even church tombstones were suddenly adorned with Vincent Connare’s cartoon lettering."),
      createBlock("Comic Sans is not inherently a bad font for its original intended use case. It was simply thrust into solemn, serious contexts where its playful informality felt actively insulting and absurd. It stands today as the ultimate cautionary tale in typographic decorum—a reminder that a typeface is essentially a tone of voice, and you shouldn't use a clown's voice at a funeral.")
